<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Han Xin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/196-bce">196 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Han Xin was the leading general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> in the Chu–Han war that founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. Having deserted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a>, he took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> heartland in 206 BCE, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-bao">Wei Bao</a> in 205 BCE by crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> on rafts, conquered Zhao and Qi, and made himself King of Qi in 203 BCE. He helped destroy Xiang Yu at Gaixia in 202 BCE, but was demoted and executed for alleged treason in 196 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/han-xin.jpg" alt="Han Xin" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B1%89%E6%B7%AE%E9%98%B4%E4%BE%AF%E9%9F%A9%E4%BF%A1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Han Xin was born in Huaiyin, in the old state of Chu, in 231 BCE. He was very poor, could not obtain a government post or become a merchant, and often relied on others for his meals.</p><p>In 209 BCE he joined Xiang Liang's rebel army against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> and afterwards served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a>, Xiang Liang's nephew. He held only the post of langzhong, and Xiang Yu ignored the strategies he proposed. In 206 BCE he deserted and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At first he received no important role, and he was nearly executed for breaking military law before Xiahou Ying spared him and recommended him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>. In late 206 BCE Han Xin had soldiers pretend to repair the gallery roads while another army secretly passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chencang">Chencang</a> and surprised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-han">Zhang Han</a>. This stratagem later became one of the Thirty-Six Stratagems.</p><p>In 205 BCE Han Xin and others captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-bao">Wei Bao</a>, King of Wei. In 204 BCE he asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> for 30,000 troops to attack Yan and Zhao in the north and Qi in the east. In Qi he defeated the combined armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-guang">Tian Guang</a> and the Chu general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-ju">Long Ju</a>, who was killed. In 203 BCE he asked to be made Acting King of Qi. Liu Bang was angry, but Zhang Liang and Chen Ping urged him to agree, and he appointed Han Xin King of Qi in full.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> died in 202 BCE, Han Xin sheltered the Chu general Zhongli Mo and refused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>'s order to arrest him. Liu Bang, now Emperor Gaozu, feared his influence and demoted him to Marquis of Huaiyin in late 202 BCE. Around 197 BCE Han Xin is said to have promised Chen Xi help from inside the capital if he rose in revolt. When Chen Xi rebelled, Han Xin stayed behind, claiming illness. In early 196 BCE he was accused of taking part in the rebellion and executed in the Hall of Zhong, Changle Palace, on Empress Lü's orders, and his three clans were exterminated.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> judged that Han Xin would have rivalled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a> had he been more modest. Several Chinese idioms come from his life, including the shame of crawling between someone's legs and the saying that with Han Xin the more soldiers the better.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Han Xin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After <b>Han Xin</b> defeated Zhao at Jingxing in 204 BCE, Liu Bang made Zhang Er King of Zhao.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-changshan">Kingdom of Changshan</a></li><li><blockquote>In 205 BCE <b>Han Xin</b> defeated and captured Xia Yue, and in 204 BCE he destroyed the Zhao army at Jingxing, where Chen Yu was killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dai-206-bc">Kingdom of Dai (206 BC)</a></li><li><blockquote>With the chancellor Xiao He managing supplies and the general <b>Han Xin</b> commanding the army, the kingdom became a base for war.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-han-206-bc">Kingdom of Han (206 BC)</a></li><li><blockquote>Later in 206 BCE Liu Bang's general <b>Han Xin</b> crossed the mountains by the Chencang route and defeated Zhang Han.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-yong">Kingdom of Yong</a></li><li><blockquote>In 203 BCE, as Liu Bang's envoy Li Yiji negotiated Qi's submission, <b>Han Xin</b> attacked anyway, routed the relief army of Long Ju on the Wei River and captured Tian Guang.</blockquote><a href="/state/qi-206-bc">Qi (206 BC)</a></li><li><blockquote>That autumn <b>Han Xin</b> feinted at one ford while crossing at Xiayang on rafts of wooden jars, and captured Wei Bao.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-wei-206-bc">Western Wei (206 BC)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Huai'an, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.59,119.02&amp;year=-206" aria-label="Huai'an on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7589 945 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Han Xin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8039 1215h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8039 1215h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.59,119.02&amp;year=-206">What was here in 206 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
